<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Menu style Classic+ on an MCC-only install (1.1.3, the Classic+ audit): the Xbox's network screens as
     Classic+ has them on the Xbox's files, as classic/xbox_screens.xml has the others - System Link's
     SELECT PROFILE (start to join), the System Link list (SYSTEM LINK GAMES: where JOIN LAN lists the
     games, where the INTERNET browser's join and CREATE LAN / CREATE INTERNET go on to the new game's
     map list) and the network game's lobby (ENLISTED PLAYERS), with the map list's and SELECT
     GAMETYPE's wrappers, whose A goes on to the next. MCC's ui.map (Halo PC's) has none of these
     three screens, and its two wrappers have no A (Halo PC's code went on from them): without these
     Classic+ opened the Halo 3 set's join screen, LAN browser and lobby for them (the owner, Nov 15:
     "need to use the old menus for the classic plus not a mixture").
     Each cp/xb/<path> here is laid out as the Xbox's ui\shell\main_menu\<path> (its place, size,
     flags, list, children in the order the game's code reads them, and the game's own functions on
     its events), from the Xbox's widgets dumped with HALO_MENUS_DUMP_TAGS. The pictures, fonts and
     words are the player's MCC ui.map's where it has them (the gradient, list_item_bkd, list_field,
     colors_sm, console_grafix, controller_graphix, the map and game type pictures, the large and small
     fonts, ui\multiplayer_game_text, the button key's words, the lobby's heading, button key, team
     spinner and players' rows); where it has not, the port's own: the start-to-join quarters, the Y
     button and the lobby's fields (tools/classic_menu_art.py), the SYSTEM LINK GAMES heading (set in
     the letters of the player's ui.map's headings as the menus load, menu_titles.c; the picture here
     the fallback), and the list's few words (CREATE GAME, the looking-for-games line, the game's
     description's labels). No Bungie picture or text is in this folder.
     menu_tags.c xbox_screen_widgets_apply makes each the definition of the map's tag of that path
     (the three stand-ins, the two wrappers) while Menu style is Classic+; the rest go by their cp/xb
     names. On the Xbox's files, and in the other styles, none of this is used. -->
